# Local Dev Stack

The canonical reference for what runs where during {{PROJECT_NAME}} local development. Update this doc and `scripts/dev-doctor.mjs` (config in `.local-stack.json`) together when the stack changes.

## Port allocation

<!-- Update this table to match `.local-stack.json` ports -->

| Port | Owner | Where it runs | Notes |
|------|-------|---------------|-------|
| 3000 | `api` | host (PM2 / `tsx watch`) | NEVER let a Docker container bind here too |
| 5432 | `postgres` | Docker container | Tracked in `docker-compose.yml` |

Any duplicate listener on these ports is a bug. `pnpm doctor` enforces this.

## What `docker compose up -d` brings up

By default: **only data services (postgres, redis, etc.)**. Any service whose dev runtime ALSO runs on the host (the API server, admin UI, workers) MUST be gated behind a Compose profile so it does NOT auto-start.

To bring up the containerized version of an app for full-stack tests:

```
docker compose --profile <profile-name> up -d
```

Always tear it down before going back to dev:

```
docker compose --profile <profile-name> stop <service>
```

## Why the profile gate exists

When a containerized version of an app and the host dev runtime listen on the same port simultaneously, the OS routes requests arbitrarily between them. The host process serves the latest source; the container serves whatever was baked into the image last build. Symptoms:

- Bug appears intermittently, indistinguishable in logs.
- Survives `pm2 restart` and orphan-process kills.
- Often surfaces as schema drift errors (`column "X" does not exist`) when the image was built before a recent rename.

The profile gate prevents this by default-disabling the containerized variant during dev.

## Process boot sequence

```
docker compose up -d                # data services only
pnpm db:migrate                     # apply migrations against local DB
pm2 start <ecosystem.cjs>           # host runtime (api + admin in separate processes)
```

## Diagnostic command

```
pnpm doctor
```

Surfaces:

- Port collisions on the canonical ports above
- Any forbidden container running outside its profile gate
- Local Docker images >14d old (likely stale builds backing future surprises)
- PM2 processes — confirms expected online, flags legacy combined wrappers
- docker-compose drift: declared image vs running image

Exit 1 on any RED check. Run before `pnpm dev` if anything feels off.

## Adding a new containerized service

1. Define it in `docker-compose.yml` with a clear comment about whether it is on by default or behind a profile.
2. If it binds a host port, add the port to `.local-stack.json` (key `ports`) and to this doc's table.
3. If it duplicates a service that runs on the host, gate it behind a profile by default.
4. If it accumulates locally-built images, add a pattern to `staleImagePatterns` in `.local-stack.json`.
5. Run `pnpm doctor` before pushing the change to confirm no port collision.

## Cleanup playbook

When `pnpm doctor` flags stale state:

| Symptom | Fix |
|---------|-----|
| Stale image >14d | `docker rmi <image>` (rebuild on next `--profile <name> up`) |
| Container running outside profile | `docker compose stop <svc> && docker compose rm -f <svc>` |
| Multiple listeners on a port | `pnpm doctor` shows pids; `taskkill /F /PID <pid>` (Windows) or `kill -9 <pid>` (mac/linux). |
| Legacy combined PM2 wrapper | `pm2 delete <legacy-name>` and migrate to per-process ecosystem |
| docker-compose drift | reconcile: update yaml OR `docker compose pull && up -d` |
